<!-- doc/src/sgml/otel-postgres-tracing.sgml -->

<sect1 id="otel-postgres-tracing" xreflabel="otel_postgres_tracing">
 <title>otel_postgres_tracing &mdash; query-level OpenTelemetry spans</title>

 <indexterm zone="otel-postgres-tracing">
  <primary>otel_postgres_tracing</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> module is the
  query-instrumentation half of
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s built-in OpenTelemetry
  trace-context support.  It installs executor and utility hooks
  that produce one OtelSpan per top-level statement, captures
  <function>ereport</function> events as span events on the active
  span, and threads parallel-worker spans onto the leader's span.
  It is a consumer of the <xref linkend="otel"/> module's
  <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> rendezvous interface;
  all cross-module work goes through that function-pointer table.
 </para>

 <para>
  Span emission is gated by a sampler decision: spans are produced
  only when a client-supplied trace context is propagated (via the
  <literal>'M'</literal> protocol header,
  <varname>otel.traceparent</varname>, or sqlcommenter), or when
  <varname>otel.trace_all_queries</varname> is on.  This module
  does not itself ship a wire-format exporter; an exporter
  loadable module &mdash; in-tree
  <filename>otel_demo_exporter</filename> or out-of-tree &mdash;
  registers an emit callback through the
  <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> and translates the
  emitted spans into OTLP, log lines, or any other format it
  wants.
 </para>

 <sect2 id="otel-postgres-tracing-loading">
  <title>Loading the module</title>

  <para>
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> must be loaded via
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/>, immediately
   after <filename>otel</filename>:
  </para>

<programlisting>
# postgresql.conf
shared_preload_libraries = 'otel,otel_postgres_tracing'
</programlisting>

  <para>
   Order matters.  <filename>otel</filename> publishes the
   <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> rendezvous variable at
   its <function>_PG_init</function>;
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> looks it up at its
   own <function>_PG_init</function>.  If the order is reversed or
   if <filename>otel</filename> is missing,
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename>'s
   <function>_PG_init</function> emits <literal>FATAL</literal>.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-postgres-tracing-configuration-parameters">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <varname>otel.trace_all_queries</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>otel.trace_all_queries</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on, every top-level statement produces a span, even if
      no client-supplied trace context was propagated.  The
      backend synthesises a fresh trace_id for the orphan span.
      Off by default; the common case is to trace only what the
      client explicitly opted into.
     </para>
     <para>
      Useful for development, ad-hoc diagnostic sessions, or
      always-on capture in environments where the application
      doesn't yet propagate trace context.  Imposes a per-statement
      span-construction cost on every backend.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-postgres-tracing-span-shape">
  <title>What spans are produced</title>

  <para>
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> emits one span per
   top-level statement:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <literal>ExecutorStart</literal> &rarr;
     <literal>ExecutorEnd</literal> for ordinary DML / SELECT
     statements.  Span name <literal>pgsql.execute</literal>;
     <literal>kind = SERVER</literal>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <literal>ProcessUtility_hook</literal> for utility commands
     (BEGIN/COMMIT, DDL, COPY, EXPLAIN, etc.).  Span name is the
     statement's command tag (e.g. <literal>BEGIN</literal>,
     <literal>CREATE TABLE</literal>);
     <literal>kind = SERVER</literal>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Nested executor invocations (PL/pgSQL function bodies,
     triggers, IMMUTABLE-function planner-time invocations) do
     NOT each get their own span by default; the outermost
     statement owns the span and nested invocations are subsumed
     into it.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Span attributes follow OpenTelemetry semantic conventions
   where possible: <literal>db.system</literal>,
   <literal>db.name</literal>, <literal>db.statement</literal>,
   <literal>db.user</literal>, <literal>net.peer.addr</literal>,
   <literal>application_name</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Statement spans opt in to
   <literal>OTEL_UNWIND_ERROR</literal>: if the executor unwinds
   via <function>ereport(ERROR)</function> before
   <literal>ExecutorEnd</literal>, the span is finalized with
   <literal>OTEL_STATUS_ERROR</literal> and a descriptive reason
   rather than being dropped.  Aborted queries appear in traces.
  </para>

  <para>
   <function>ereport</function> events at WARNING level or higher
   that fire during an active span are captured as span events
   (with <literal>sqlstate</literal>,
   <literal>filename</literal>, <literal>lineno</literal>,
   <literal>message</literal>, <literal>detail</literal>,
   <literal>hint</literal>).  An ERROR-level ereport also sets
   the span's status to <literal>OTEL_STATUS_ERROR</literal>.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-postgres-tracing-parallel">
  <title>Parallel-query workers</title>

  <para>
   Parallel workers each emit their own statement span, with
   <literal>parent_span_id</literal> set to the leader's span_id
   rather than the client-supplied root span_id.  The leader
   publishes its span identity to a per-backend shared-memory
   slot at span-start; workers read the slot at their own
   span-start.  See <xref linkend="otel-parallel-workers"/>.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-postgres-tracing-exporters">
  <title>Span emission and exporters</title>

  <para>
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> emits spans through
   the <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname>'s
   <literal>span_emit</literal> dispatch.  Registered exporter
   hooks (set via
   <literal>api-&gt;register_emit_hook</literal>) receive each
   span as an in-memory <structname>OtelSpan</structname>.  Two
   in-tree exporters demonstrate the pattern:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The JSON-log fallback emitter that ships with
     <filename>otel</filename>, controlled by
     <varname>otel.emit_spans_to_log</varname>.  Emits each span
     as a structured LOG line; useful for development and for
     deployments where an out-of-tree exporter is not feasible.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>otel_demo_exporter</filename>, a minimal
     file-based exporter that writes one JSON line per span to a
     configurable path.  Demonstrates how an out-of-tree
     exporter registers against the rendezvous API.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Out-of-tree exporters &mdash; including SDK-backed exporters
   such as <ulink url="https://github.com/ringerc/postgres_otel_tracing_demo">postgres_otel_tracing_demo</ulink>
   (Rust, opentelemetry-rust SDK) &mdash; consume the same
   rendezvous interface.  Adding an exporter does not require
   modifying <filename>otel</filename> or
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename>.
  </para>
 </sect2>
</sect1>
